# Aside

<h2 id="purpose">Purpose</h2>

The HTML `aside` element holds content that relates to the main content but is not part of its main point: background, a related topic or a short note. People should be able to skip it and still do what they came to do.

This page owns the `aside` element. Home Assistant has no aside component. For content people must read, use an [alert](alert-and-tip-callout.html) or a [warning callout](warning-callout.html). For a short passage set apart inside the text, see [Inset text](inset-text.html).

This page covers:

- Aside
  - `<aside>`

<h2 id="anatomy">Anatomy</h2>

An aside holds ordinary content, starting with a heading that says what it relates to, such as “About this setting”. It sits beside or after the main content.

Its role depends on where it is. Browsers treat it as a complementary landmark when it is not inside another section, and may treat it as a plain block when it is inside one and has no name. Do not assume it is always a landmark. It has no theme variables.

<h2 id="variants">Variants, variables and extensibility</h2>

<h3 id="child-aside"><code>&lt;aside&gt;</code></h3>

It accepts only the global attributes. When you use it as a landmark, name it:

```html
<aside aria-labelledby="about-title">
  <h2 id="about-title">About this setting</h2>
  <p>The hot water schedule only changes when the boiler is on.</p>
</aside>
```

**In a Home Assistant Markdown card.** The card allows `aside`, but drops every attribute, so an `aria-label`, `aria-labelledby` or `id` is removed. You cannot name an aside there. Start it with a heading and keep it short.

<h2 id="limits">Advantages and limitations</h2>

- It marks supporting content, so software and people can skip it.
- It needs no integration.
- Different browsers and screen readers treat it differently, so do not rely on it for navigation.
- In a Markdown card it cannot be named.
- It is easy to overuse: anything people must read is not an aside.

<h2 id="usage">Usage policy and cognitive accessibility</h2>

Keep an aside short and say what it relates to. Never put a required instruction, a warning, an error or a live device state in it. Put it after the main content in the source order, so on a narrow screen (320 CSS pixels) it stacks below instead of coming first. Beside the content on a wide screen it needs ordinary layout styles.

Success criteria that apply:

- 1.3.1 Info and Relationships and 1.3.2 Meaningful Sequence: the aside follows the content it relates to.
- 2.4.6 Headings and Labels: the heading says what it is about.
- 2.4.1 Bypass Blocks: a named landmark can help, but it is not guaranteed.
- 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value: name it when you use it as a landmark.
- 1.3.6 Identify Purpose (AAA).

Short supporting text with a clear heading keeps the effort low.

